[s3;:1: 1. SculptorCtrl`'s Architecture&]
[s5;~~~2752; SculptorCtrl widget acts as the bridge between the  
U`+`+ UI event loop and the OpenGL pipeline. To use it effectively, 
you must understand three crucial design patterns:&]
[s22;:1`.1: 1.1. The Separation of Data and Context&]
[s5; It is important to distinguish between CPU`-side data preparation 
and GPU`-side context operations:&]
[s5;i150;O0; [* The Constructor]: You are completely free to load textures, 
attach them to your [C^topic`:`/`/SculptorCtrl`/src`/Upp`_SculptorCtrl`_ModelGL`_en`-us`#Upp`:`:ModelGL^ M
odelGL] objects, generate vertex data, and configure U`+`+ timers 
right in the constructor. [C^topic`:`/`/SculptorCtrl`/src`/Upp`_SculptorCtrl`_ModelGL`_en`-us`#Upp`:`:ModelGL^ M
odelGL] handles this data gracefully before the GPU is even involved.&]
[s5;i150;O0; [* The Context Callback]: Operations that explicitly require 
the underlying OpenGL context (like compiling GLSL shaders via 
[C^topic`:`/`/SculptorCtrl`/src`/Upp`_SculptorCtrl`_en`-us`#Upp`:`:SculptorCtrl`:`:AddShader`(const String`&`,const String`&`,const String`&`)^ A
ddShader()]) cannot happen until the OS window and context actually 
exist. [C^topic`:`/`/SculptorCtrl`/src`/Upp`_SculptorCtrl`_en`-us`#Upp`:`:SculptorCtrl`:`:InitScene`(`)^ I
nitScene()] is automatically fired by the framework the moment 
that context becomes active.&]
[s5;i150;O0; [* Dynamic Changes]: While [C^topic`:`/`/SculptorCtrl`/src`/Upp`_SculptorCtrl`_en`-us`#Upp`:`:SculptorCtrl`:`:InitScene`(`)^ I
nitScene()] is the first place the OpenGL context is available, 
it is not the only place you can manipulate it. You can dynamically 
add, set, or remove shaders and post`-processors at any point 
later in your application`'s lifecycle.&]
[s22;:1`.2: 1.2. The GLScene&]
[s5; The [C^topic`:`/`/SculptorCtrl`/src`/Upp`_SculptorCtrl`_en`-us`#Upp`:`:SculptorCtrl`:`:Render`(GLScene`&`)^ R
ender()] method fires every time the window repaints. The [C^topic`:`/`/SculptorCtrl`/src`/Upp`_SculptorCtrl`_GLScene`_en`-us`#Upp`:`:GLScene^ G
LScene] object is your master control panel, so to speak. Instead 
of writing raw OpenGL calls, you route everything through [C^topic`:`/`/SculptorCtrl`/src`/Upp`_SculptorCtrl`_GLScene`_en`-us`#Upp`:`:GLScene^ G
LScene]. It handles:&]
[s5;i150;O0; [* Camera Matrices]: [C^topic`:`/`/SculptorCtrl`/src`/Upp`_SculptorCtrl`_GLScene`_en`-us`#Upp`:`:GLScene`:`:Perspective`(float`,float`,float`,float`)^ P
erspective()] and [C^topic`:`/`/SculptorCtrl`/src`/Upp`_SculptorCtrl`_GLScene`_en`-us`#Upp`:`:GLScene`:`:LookAt`(const Point3D`&`,const Point3D`&`,const Point3D`&`)^ L
ookAt()] configure the lens and view.&]
[s5;i150;O0; [* Global Lighting]: [C^topic`:`/`/SculptorCtrl`/src`/Upp`_SculptorCtrl`_GLScene`_en`-us`#Upp`:`:GLScene`:`:AddLight`(const Point3D`&`,const RGBA`&`)^ A
ddLight()] feeds directional light data into the active shader.&]
[s5;i150;O0; [* State Machine]: [C^topic`:`/`/SculptorCtrl`/src`/Upp`_SculptorCtrl`_GLScene`_en`-us`#Upp`:`:GLScene`:`:Cull`(bool`)^ C
ull()], [C^topic`:`/`/SculptorCtrl`/src`/Upp`_SculptorCtrl`_GLScene`_en`-us`#Upp`:`:GLScene`:`:Shading`(bool`)^ S
hading()], and [C^topic`:`/`/SculptorCtrl`/src`/Upp`_SculptorCtrl`_GLScene`_en`-us`#Upp`:`:GLScene`:`:UseShader`(const String`&`)^ U
seShader()] instruct the GPU how to process the next draw call.&]
[s22;:1`.3: 1.3. Matrix Isolation&]
[s5; When you translate or rotate an object in 3D space, you are 
mutating the global matrix stack. If you rotate the world to 
draw a spinning ball, the matrix remains rotated for whatever 
you draw next.&]
[s5; Historically, OpenGL handled this by providing[C  ][C@5 glPushMatrix()] 
and [C@5 glPopMatrix()] functions to temporarily save and restore 
the matrix state. While[C^topic`:`/`/SculptorCtrl`/src`/Upp`_SculptorCtrl`_GLScene`_en`-us`#Upp`:`:GLScene^  
GLScene] exposes these exact same functions as methods, manually 
pairing them is notoriously error`-prone. A single missed `"pop`" 
will corrupt the camera for the rest of the application`'s lifecycle.&]
[s5; The native and preferred approach in U`+`+ is utilizing RAII. 
By declaring a [^topic`:`/`/SculptorCtrl`/src`/Upp`_SculptorCtrl`_GLScene`_en`-us`#Upp`:`:GLScene`:`:Scope^ G
LScene`::Scope] at the top of a rendering function, you tell the 
framework to automatically push the matrix stack upon creation. 
When the function returns and the scope object is destroyed, 
the matrix is guaranteed to be popped back to its original configuration.&]
[s0;b42;a42;ph [/ Note: This protects the matrix mutations, not global 
OpenGL states like culling or shader bindings, which you still 
need to set explicitly per draw call!]&]
[s3;:2: 2. The Mathematics of the Illusion&]
[s5;~~~3648; Before we write the code, we need to understand the 
math that makes this demo look crisp.&]
[s22;:2`.1: 2.1. Anti`-Aliased Grid (Fragment Shader)&]
[s5; If we used a standard image texture for the room`'s grid, it 
would look blurry up close and jagged in the distance. Instead, 
we calculate the lines mathematically:&]
[s5;i150;O0; We use [C@5 fract(uv)] to break the floor into repeating 
[C@5 0.0] to [C@5 1.0] coordinate cells.&]
[s5;i150;O0; To draw the grid lines perfectly crisp at any distance, 
we use screen`-space derivatives ([C@5 fwidth]). This GLSL function 
tells the GPU exactly how fast the texture coordinates are changing 
from one screen pixel to the next. By feeding this into [C@5 smoothstep()], 
the lines dynamically scale their thickness to remain perfectly 
anti`-aliased, preventing moiré patterns on the horizon.&]
[s22;:2`.2: 2.2. Checkerboard Pattern (Fragment Shader)&]
[s5; Wrapping a 2D checkerboard texture around a 3D sphere causes 
ugly `"pinching`" at the poles and a visible seam down the back. 
We bypass this entirely by using spherical math:&]
[s5;i150;O0; We convert the 3D vertex position ([C@5 x], [C@5 y], [C@5 z]) 
into spherical angles (azimuth and inclination).&]
[s5;i150;O0; We map these angles into continuous 3D space by multiplying 
two sine waves: [C@5 sin(u) `* sin(v)]. Where the result is positive, 
we color it red; where negative, white. Because sine waves are 
continuous and periodic, the checkerboard wraps the sphere flawlessly 
with zero seams.&]
[s22;:2`.3: 2.3. The Bouncing Physics (Loop)&]
[s5; A realistic bounce requires sharp impacts, not a smooth, floating 
wave.&]
[s5;i150;O0; A standard [C@5 sin(t)] function creates a smooth wave 
drifting between [C@5 `-1] and [C@5 1].&]
[s5;i150;O0; By wrapping it in an absolute value function ([C@5 abs(sin(t))]) 
we flip the bottom half of the wave upwards. This creates a series 
of perfect parabolas with sharp `"V`" shapes at the bottom, mimicking 
a hard floor impact.&]
[s5;i150;O0; We scale this by [C@5 4.5] and offset it by [C@5 `-2.5] 
to ensure the bottom edge of the sphere hits exactly at the [C@5 y 
`= `-4.5] floor coordinate.&]
[s3;:3: 3. Assembling the Demo&]
[s5; Now that the architecture and math established, let`'s assemble 
the U`+`+ application step`-by`-step.&]
[s22;:3`.1: 3.1. Generate the Scene Geometry&]
